From: Kettle Linux
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: qcom: sm8550/sm8250-lpass-lpi: force runtime suspend across system sleep

The same change as 1462 (Armada 0565) makes for sm8650-lpass-lpi. The
LPI pin controller only has runtime PM ops and is a device-link
supplier of the SoundWire controllers. When a SoundWire peripheral is
kept runtime-active (the WCD938x, held for jack detection), the
controllers are force-suspended for system sleep but keep their link
references, so the pin controller stays active with its LPASS_HW_MACRO
and LPASS_HW_DCODEC votes, and the ADSP keeps LPASS (and CX) up through
s2idle. Force the runtime suspend across system sleep on SM8550 (AYN
Portal/Thor) and SM8250 (Retroid Pocket 5) too.
---
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-sm8550-lpass-lpi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-sm8550-lpass-lpi.c
@@ -213,6 +213,7 @@
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, lpi_pinctrl_of_match);
 
 static const struct dev_pm_ops lpi_pinctrl_pm_ops = {
+	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend, pm_runtime_force_resume)
 	RUNTIME_PM_OPS(pm_clk_suspend, pm_clk_resume, NULL)
 };
 
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-sm8250-lpass-lpi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-sm8250-lpass-lpi.c
@@ -137,6 +137,7 @@
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, lpi_pinctrl_of_match);
 
 static const struct dev_pm_ops lpi_pinctrl_pm_ops = {
+	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend, pm_runtime_force_resume)
 	RUNTIME_PM_OPS(pm_clk_suspend, pm_clk_resume, NULL)
 };
 
